{"essay": "When Professor Catlove opens cat food, his cats\u2019 behavior\u2014purring, meowing, rubbing\u2014illustrates classical conditioning; the can\u2019s sound is a conditioned stimulus predicting food. Operant conditioning appears as repeated approach and vocalization, positively reinforced by receiving food. Social learning may emerge if kittens mimic older cats\u2019 responses, while Professor Catlove\u2019s morning routine reflects conditioned habits.\n\n**References:**\n\n1. Skinner, B. F. (1938). The Behavior of Organisms: An Experimental Analysis. New York: Appleton-Century.\n2. Pavlov, I. P. (1927). Conditioned Reflexes: An Investigation of the Physiological Activity of the Cerebral Cortex. London: Oxford University Press.\n3. Bandura, A. (1977). Social Learning Theory. Englewood Cliffs, NJ: Prentice-Hall.\n4. Thorndike, E. L. (1911). Animal Intelligence: Experimental Studies. New York: Macmillan.\n5. Yin, H. H., & Knowlton, B. J. (2006). The role of the basal ganglia in habit formation. *Nature Reviews Neuroscience, 7*(6), 464-476."}